--TEST--
Core\Str::indexOf saturates a `from` outside the subject where strpos raises a ValueError, and {before} bounds where a match ends
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
ADR 0063 R8's sign rule reads every position through `granularity::Unit::byte_of_signed_index`, which saturates at either end so that a search starting past the subject finds nothing — composable in a loop where PHP's ValueError is not — and `lastIndexOf`'s `before` names the end of the window rather than `strrpos`'s bound on where a match may start.
--FILE--
<?mwl
final class Show {
    public static function render(?uint $found): string {
        if ($found == null) {
            return "none";
        }
        return $found as string;
    }
}

// "haystack" is 8 units long. PHP's `strpos($h, $n, 20)` and
// `strpos($h, $n, -20)` are both `ValueError: Argument #3 ($offset) must be
// contained in argument #1`; MWL saturates instead, so a `from` past the end is
// a search of nothing and one past the front is a search of everything — and
// an empty needle saturates with it, landing at the end rather than at 20.
echo Show::render(Core\Str::indexOf("haystack", "st", {from: 20})), "\n";
echo Show::render(Core\Str::indexOf("haystack", "st", {from: -20})), "\n";
echo Show::render(Core\Str::indexOf("haystack", "", {from: 20})), "\n";

// The same saturation on the mirror member, and then the shape difference that
// makes `before` untranslatable into `strrpos`'s third argument: PHP's negative
// offset admits an occurrence that *starts* at or before `strlen + offset`, so
// `strrpos("abcabcabc", "abc", -3)` is 6, while `{before: -3}` admits one that
// *ends* at or before unit 6 and is therefore 3.
echo Show::render(Core\Str::lastIndexOf("abcabcabc", "abc", {before: -3})), "\n";
echo Show::render(Core\Str::lastIndexOf("abcabcabc", "abc", {before: 20})), "\n";
echo Show::render(Core\Str::lastIndexOf("abcabcabc", "abc", {before: -20})), "\n";
--EXPECT--
none
3
8
3
6
none
